{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792499687,"nonce":"8ExemImuQygDmA04Iq/dCD+f4jo=","message":"Landed in 63f980f9020ebb03f99f52b7d015e32d85382823.\n\nDense number[], boolean[], string[] and Array\u003cT\u003e now use shared native list headers and element GEPs. Length and push use the compiler's inline list operations. Nested generic array parameters/returns feed shared cached HIR monomorphization; repeated concrete calls do not create duplicate instances or dynamic boxed access. TypeScript number remains f64. Canonical array loops use i64 induction, converting counter values to number only where the source needs them.\n\nBounds follow storage identity through aliases, literal sizes, returned-array summaries, length guards and canonical loops. Calls with argument binding writes cannot reuse alias/static-lifetime summaries; guard facts are not replayed across binding writes, and index facts are checked after evaluating the receiver. Receiver/index capture consumes the existing AST instead of deep cloning it. Proof identities are integer handles, and binding-write range queries use one sorted offset cache.\n\nShared lowering now gives growable-array signatures the same List layout as SSA. Nested array/index operand continuations settle before following instructions. Element loads/stores use natural alignment, including one-byte booleans after generic substitution. Monomorphization substitution maps use FxHashMap without temporary map conversions.\n\nValidation on macOS ARM64 with LLVM 21:\n- All 41 zyntax_ts tests pass both with default features and with llvm-backend.\n- Six new array tests cover optimized HIR GEPs and CLIF loads/multiply without per-index float-to-int conversions; native i64, f64 and string-pointer access at nonzero indices; cached instances; one header/buffer release through returned aliases; interpreter-first execution; and mixed Cranelift/LLVM promotion. Boolean specialization checks one-byte load alignment.\n- 64 relevant compiler integration tests pass: list_header_casts, expression_lowering_tests, pattern_matching_tests, union_abi, implicit_aggregate_return and osr_tier_transfer.\n- 11 monomorphization and 3 const-evaluation unit tests pass.\n- Seven active ZynML GEP/layout regressions pass. Four existing probes remain ignored by their own annotations.\n- Clippy with warnings denied passes for all TS targets and the compiler library with LLVM; cargo fmt and diff checks pass.\n- Live TypeScript 6.0.2/Node comparison: 194 cases across interpreter, baseline and LLVM profiles, 579 PASS / 3 XFAIL. All 15 new array cases pass on every profile; pending/arrays is removed from KNOWN_FAILURES. The remaining XFAILs are the existing inferred-union case. Binary hash stayed unchanged throughout the live run; no concurrent Cargo builds.\n\nRemaining compatibility work stays under parent 6875fc2949f83d682861ecab814e0ff82c824f5990620b0d64ca0340ebb026e5: potentially undefined indexes, sparse/length changes, other array methods and receivers, and ownership for non-static string/reference containers and generic construction/mutation. These remain explicit Unsupported gaps. No runtime speed benchmark was used to claim a speedup here.","files":null}]}